# session/exclude: windows usher never captures and never places.
#
# MOSTLY EMPTY ON PURPOSE, and it did not start that way. The goal is pixel
# perfect placement of every window you left open, so an exception here is a
# window usher gives up on. Two changes removed the reason for nearly all of
# them:
#
#  - AGGRESSIVE vs STEADY. Placement is aggressive only for a while after
#    session start (or an `aggressive` kick); after that ONLY session/include
#    anchors are placed. So a window that opens during normal use is left
#    exactly where it opens, and the whack-a-mole these rules were written to
#    stop cannot happen in the first place.
#  - A WINDOW ITS PLUGIN CANNOT IDENTIFY IS NOT LEARNED. Chrome keys a window
#    by its SessionID, not by the page title in the titlebar, and when that id
#    cannot be resolved usher stores nothing at all rather than falling back to
#    the title. A transient TITLE can therefore never become a remembered slot,
#    which is the whole of what the Chrome rules below used to prevent.
#
# Those Chrome rules are gone: ^New Tab$, ^New Incognito Tab$, ^Google Chrome$,
# ^Sign in, ^Settings -.*. Each named a title a Chrome window wears for a
# moment, and a title is no longer a key. A Settings window you leave open now
# comes back where you left it, which is the point of the whole program.
#
# WHAT IS STILL WORTH EXCLUDING is a different class, and the distinction is
# the useful part: not "a window we know, in a transient state", but "an app
# usher has no handle for at all". An app with no plugin can only be keyed by
# its title, so an app whose title is really a document name mints one dead
# store entry per document. That is what the one rule below is.
#
# One rule per line, always the same shape:
#
#   <app-regex> :: <title-regex>
#
# BOTH fields are required. Each is a Python regex matched with re.search, so
# it is UNANCHORED (a substring test) -- add ^...$ to pin it, exactly as the
# work boundary's own ^\[WORK does. Use .* for "any" in either field:
#
#   .* :: ^New Tab$          any app, whose title is exactly "New Tab"
#   ^google-chrome$ :: .*    every google-chrome window
#
# A window matching ANY rule here is never recorded and never moved. A line
# beginning with # is a comment; blank lines are ignored. `session-mgr exclude`
# prints the rules as loaded and flags any bad line. Edits apply AUTOMATICALLY:
# the running watcher re-reads this file within a second of saving.
#
# NOTE: the work/personal boundary (titles starting "[WORK") is NOT here,
# because it
# is a hardcoded safety wall inside usher, not a tunable. Never rely on this
# file for it.

# An image viewer titles its window with the FILENAME, so every picture you
# open would become its own store entry pointing at a place. No plugin claims
# it, so there is no better handle to key on; excluding it is the honest
# answer rather than remembering a thousand filenames.
org.gnome.Loupe :: ^.*$
